  cites Safari chapter World
  cites Safari chapter Render
  cites Judge chapter Grade

 Render: one frame's collection -- every kind gathered, capped, and merged into a
 single depth order.

 THE MERGE ORDER IS THE ZIG'S ARRAY ORDER and the sort is stable, so it is also
 the order equal depths come out in: trees, towers, billboards, cats, the truck,
 then the rails. That is why `order`'s length must be the six lists' lengths
 summed -- nothing is dropped at the merge and nothing is added.

 EVERY KIND IS CAPPED SEPARATELY, and the caps are the zig's fixed arrays. On this
 route none of them is reached, which the chapter's own prose says; grading that
 the collection is UNDER each cap is the honest claim, because a spec asserting
 the caps fire would be asserting something the route cannot produce.

 THE TWO CULL COUNTS ARE DIFFERENT MEASUREMENTS AND ARE BOTH REPORTED. `cull-seg`
 is how many animals were never placed because their segment was out of the farm
 reach -- a count taken from the route, without projecting anything. `cull-size`
 is how many were placed and then found too small. A chapter reporting one number
 for both would lose the distinction between "too far to bother with" and "close
 enough to try and not worth drawing".

 We say:

Section: The Frame

 THE WHOLE ROUTE, because `collect` builds its own chain by following `exit-to`
 and those are ROUTE indices -- a short list runs off the end at the first
 segment whose exit points past it.

  segs : List Segment
  segs = seg-list 0

  seg-list : Integer -> List Segment
  seg-list (i) = if i >= list-length route then [] else [segment-at i] & seg-list (i + 1)

  origin : Pose
  origin = Pose { along = 0.0, across = 0.0, yaw = 0.0, hw = 2.0 }

  frame : Collected
  frame = collect segs 0 origin 500.0 0.0 v-base 500.0

  prev-got : List Integer
  prev-got = [prev-index 0, prev-index 1, prev-index 5]

  prev-want : List Integer
  prev-want = [0, 0, 4]

Section: Nothing Is Lost At The Merge

 The depth order is exactly the six collections concatenated and sorted, so its
 length is their lengths summed -- with the truck counting one if it is present
 and none if it is not.

  merge-got : List Integer
  merge-got =
    let n = list-length frame.order
    in let parts = list-length (tree-items frame.trees 0) + list-length (tower-items frame.towers 0)
    in let more = list-length (cow-items frame.cows 0) + list-length (cat-items frame.cats 0)
    in let rest = list-length (truck-items frame.truck) + list-length (rail-items frame.rails 0)
    in [n - (parts + more + rest)]

  merge-want : List Integer
  merge-want = [0]

 It is a real frame: there is something of most kinds in it.

  present-got : List Boolean
  present-got =
    [ list-length frame.trees > 0,
      list-length frame.towers > 0,
      list-length frame.cows > 0,
      list-length frame.rails > 0,
      list-length frame.order > 0 ]

  present-want : List Boolean
  present-want = [True, True, True, True, True]

Section: Under Every Cap

 The caps are the zig's fixed arrays and this route reaches none of them, so what
 is graded is that the collection stays under each -- not that a cap fires.

  cap-got : List Boolean
  cap-got =
    [ list-length frame.trees <= max-vis-trees,
      list-length frame.towers <= max-vis-towers,
      list-length frame.cows <= max-vis-critters,
      list-length frame.cats <= max-vis-cats ]

  cap-want : List Boolean
  cap-want = [True, True, True, True]

  cap-values-got : List Integer
  cap-values-got = [max-vis-trees, max-vis-towers, max-vis-cats]

  cap-values-want : List Integer
  cap-values-want = [640, 16, 8]

Section: The Depth Order

 Far to near, so every item is at least as deep as the one after it -- to the
 sort's own resolution, which declines to order two depths the game cannot tell
 apart.

  descending : List Item, Integer -> Boolean
  descending (xs) (i) =
    if i + 1 >= list-length xs then True
    else if deeper-than (list-at xs (i + 1)).fwd (list-at xs i).fwd then False
    else descending xs (i + 1)

  sorted-got : List Boolean
  sorted-got = [descending frame.order 0, descending [] 0]

  sorted-want : List Boolean
  sorted-want = [True, True]

Section: The Two Culls

 One is counted off the route without projecting anything; the other needs the
 placement. They are different numbers about different things.

  cull-got : List Boolean
  cull-got =
    [ frame.cull-seg >= 0,
      frame.cull-size >= 0,
      frame.cull-seg > 0 ]

  cull-want : List Boolean
  cull-want = [True, True, True]

 The segment cull is exactly the animals on the chain entries past the farm
 reach, and it is arithmetic over the route rather than anything the frame did.

  seg-cull-got : List Integer
  seg-cull-got =
    let a = seg-cull-count segs [0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5] 3
    in let b = list-length (list-at segs 3).cows + list-length (list-at segs 3).pigs
    in [ a - b,
         seg-cull-count segs [0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5] 0,
         seg-cull-count segs [0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5] 2,
         walk-seg-cull segs [] 0 ]

  seg-cull-want : List Integer
  seg-cull-want = [0, 0, 0, 0]

 A shorter chain culls less, because there are fewer entries past the reach.

  shrink-got : List Boolean
  shrink-got =
    let long = walk-seg-cull segs [0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5] 0
    in let short = walk-seg-cull segs [0, 1, 2] 0
    in [long > short, short == 0]

  shrink-want : List Boolean
  shrink-want = [True, True]

Section: Entry

  opening : [Console] Nothing = act
    print-line-uni (grade-ints  "rn-prev  " prev-got prev-want)
    print-line-uni (grade-ints  "rn-merge " merge-got merge-want)
    print-line-uni (grade-bools "rn-presnt" present-got present-want)
    print-line-uni (grade-bools "rn-cap   " cap-got cap-want)
    print-line-uni (grade-ints  "rn-caps  " cap-values-got cap-values-want)
    print-line-uni (grade-bools "rn-sorted" sorted-got sorted-want)
    print-line-uni (grade-bools "rn-cull  " cull-got cull-want)
    print-line-uni (grade-ints  "rn-segcul" seg-cull-got seg-cull-want)
    print-line-uni (grade-bools "rn-shrink" shrink-got shrink-want)
  end
